        "t1": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>West of MoPac, east of I-35: Austin's deck geography</strong></p><p>Austin sits on the Balcones Escarpment, and the line runs roughly along MoPac. West of it, Westlake Hills, Rollingwood, Barton Hills and Tarrytown are built on limestone hills. The back of the house is often a deck hanging over the slope, with a view of downtown or the Hill Country. Further west, Lakeway, Bee Cave, Steiner Ranch and the Lake Travis shore add waterfront decks and pool decks with a lake view. East of I-35, East Austin and Mueller are full of modern builds with flat roofs, rooftop decks and open interior stairs. Downtown and Rainey Street condo towers have balconies that face the river. Dripping Springs and the Hill Country beyond Bee Cave are large lots with view patios. In every one of these settings, a frameless glass deck railing is chosen for the same reason: it keeps the view.</p>"
          }
        },
        "t2": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>Austin building code and who issues the permit</strong></p><p>The City of Austin applies the 2021 International Residential Code with local amendments, and residential permits go through the Development Services Department. The code sets a 36\" minimum guard height for houses, including a rooftop deck on a single-family build. It sets 42\" for commercial and multifamily buildings, which covers the downtown condo towers. For pool barriers, most cities in Central Texas apply the 48\" rule from the IRC and ISPSC. Westlake Hills, Rollingwood, Lakeway, Bee Cave and Dripping Springs are separate cities with their own building departments and their own amendments. Outside any city limits, around Lake Travis, the rules can be different again, and many communities add HOA review. Ask the office that covers your address before you order. Our <a href=\"/pages/texas-building-code-for-glass-railings\">Texas building code page</a> keeps the statewide summary current, and the <a href=\"/pages/glass-railing-texas\">Texas hub</a> links Dallas, Houston and San Antonio.</p>"
          }
        },
        "t3": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>Hillside decks, limestone and delivery up a steep driveway</strong></p><p>A hillside deck in Westlake Hills or Barton Hills is usually a wood-framed deck on posts. The railing goes on the deck framing, not into the rock. The side-mount spigot fixes to the fascia or rim joist, keeps the full deck width and suits a deck that overhangs the slope. The top-mount spigot fixes to deck boards over blocking, or straight to a concrete pool deck. Our engineering documents cover both spigots on wood and concrete; screws are sold separately. For the sun, the materials are 12 mm tempered safety glass and 2205 marine-grade stainless steel hardware with a 10-year no-rust promise. Delivery is by freight carrier with a tailgate lift, processed in 2 to 3 business days and delivered in 5 to 10 business days. A long or steep Hill Country driveway can stop a freight truck, so tell us about access when you order.</p>"
          }
        }
